{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving", "file": "references/global-propagation-reassessment.md", "content": "# Reassessing apparent propagation in noisy grids

A distinctive colored cluster embedded in a dense random-looking binary field can make a fill/propagation hypothesis feel plausible, but visual proximity is not enough. Before applying a flood fill, replay it against every demo and compare the full predicted set with all recolored and untouched cells. Test 4- and 8-connectivity separately, and check whether candidate traversable cells are actually limited to one background value. If either model predicts extensive recoloring that the demonstration does not show, reject the flood-fill premise; do not submit a broad fill merely because the test has marker cells.

For costly demo requests, make the uncertainty actionable: state the remaining candidate rule families and identify an observable difference a new pair can resolve. If the evidence remains underdetermined, obtain another example rather than making successive speculative submissions. After any incorrect answer, reconsider the whole transformation, not just individual coordinates.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving/references/global-propagation-reassessment.md"}
